And she, then, to Olympus came, to the bronze-floored house of Zeus,
and tearful upon her father’s knees sat the maiden,
and about her, then, the ambrosial fine robe trembled;

but under him
the Trojans at once were being thrown into confusion, put to flight, and no
might was arising;

But when into the wall they have drawn breath again, crowded together,
again put on the doors, closely fitted;
for I fear lest the ruinous man leap into the wall.”

And they, straight for the city and the high wall,
parched with thirst, covered with dust, out of the plain,
were fleeing;

There would have taken high-gated Troy the sons of the Achaeans,
if not Apollo Phoebus Agenor, heavenly, had urged on,
a man, Antenor’s son, blameless and strong.

and in his heart courage he cast, and beside him himself
he stood, that the heavy hands of death he might keep away,
leaning against an oak;

For indeed surely for this man the skin is woundable by sharp bronze,
and there is in him one life, and mortal him they say, men,
to be;

As a leopard goes from a deep lair
against a man, a hunter, and in no way in her heart
is afraid or frightened, when she hears the barking;

For even if, getting there first, he either stabs her or hits her,
yet even pierced through on the spear she does not cease
from her might, before she either closes with him or is overcome;

“Truly now, I suppose, greatly you hope in your wits, glorious Achilles,
on this day to sack the city of the lofty Trojans,
childish one;

for in it both many and valiant men we are,
who also in front of dear parents and wives and sons
Ilium protect;

While he him over the wheat-bearing plain was chasing,
having turned him to the river, deep-eddying Scamander,
running a little way out ahead;

And they did not dare, outside the city and the wall,
to wait any longer for one another, and to find out who had escaped
and who had died in the war;
